NVK's own mapped device-local memory that the CPU only writes stays in video memory by default outside WindowServer.
NullMoth's own change (not an upstream Mesa commit).
Device-local memory that NVK itself maps (the shader heap, descriptor tables and descriptor sets, the event and QMD
heaps, USERD, the zero page) was moved to system RAM by the NVRM back end's rule for mappable memory, uncached on the
GPU side, so the GPU fetched shader code and descriptors over PCIe. NVK_LOCAL_MAP_SYSMEM=0 kept it in video memory,
reached through BAR1, where BAR1 covers the whole VRAM; that was opt-in since a stall of WindowServer's channel was
seen with it, and only the Cyberpunk profile set it. It is now the default in every process except WindowServer, for
memory that is not shared with another process or device (exported or imported memory keeps system RAM, as it does for
application memory) and that the CPU does not read back while the app runs: shader code, the image and sampler
tables, the zero page, indirect execution sets and a linked cubin (the Metal plugin's report after a lost device does
read the image table back, once).
What the CPU reads back keeps system RAM under the default, marked NVKMD_MEM_HOST_READ where it is allocated: USERD
(GPPut is read before every doorbell, and the RM zeroes a client's USERD only in system RAM), the event heap
(vkGetEventStatus) and descriptor pools. A descriptor copy reads its source set through the map, the Metal plugin
copies unchanged descriptors on every draw, and behind BAR1 the map is write-combined, so each such read would be a
trip to the card; the plugin's pool is also 21 MB per process, which would come out of the video memory of a game.
WindowServer, the one process the stall was seen in, keeps system RAM as before. The stall has the shape of the lost
doorbell that the read of GPPut through BAR1 before the doorbell fixed later (nvkmd_nvrm_ctx.c); that the two are the
same was not shown on a card.
With USERD in system RAM that read of GPPut no longer goes through BAR1, so it no longer holds the doorbell back
until the shader code and table entries written through BAR1 ahead of it have landed, as it did in the one placement
that ran on a card (NVK_LOCAL_MAP_SYSMEM=0, where USERD is behind BAR1 too). Under the default each device therefore
keeps one page that the default places behind BAR1 mapped (nvkmd_nvrm_dev_bar_order_init) and reads a word of it
before every doorbell, after the read of GPPut: a submission, the flush that waits, and the doorbell a rebuilt or
stalled channel is rung with. Whether the GPU could fetch such data before the write landed was never seen on a card;
the read keeps the order the tested placement had, for one read through BAR1 per doorbell. Without the page (another
mode, or no room for it behind BAR1) there is no read.
NVK_LOCAL_MAP_SYSMEM=1 is the way back: system RAM in every process, as in release 3.0. NVK_LOCAL_MAP_SYSMEM=0 means
what it meant: video memory in every process, WindowServer, shared memory and what the CPU reads back included.
NVK_BAR_VRAM=0, which keeps an application's own host-visible device-local memory out of BAR1, also keeps the new
default off, because the rule would otherwise place that application memory behind BAR1 again. Where BAR1 does not
cover the VRAM nothing changes.
When the RM has no video memory for an allocation the new default placed, or refuses its map through BAR1
(nvkmd_dev_alloc_mapped_mem; the back end marks such memory NVKMD_MEM_DEFAULT_BAR), the allocation is made in system
RAM, as in release 3.0, instead of failing; one placed by NVK_LOCAL_MAP_SYSMEM=0 or by its caller still fails as
before. A refused map that is recovered this way is not reported as an error (a line under the diagnostics only).
The decision is in two functions without side effects (nvrm_local_map_rule, nvrm_local_map_wants_bar), which
tools/test_nvk_local_map.py compiles and holds, and the settings are read once for the process with util_call_once_data
instead of an unguarded static.

